00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.210
Chris: Development environments

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:07.580
So, what are development environments and
how do we go about selecting an appropriate

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.799
development environment for different languages?

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.889
A development environment helps coders
to develop a program using a set

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:17.449
of processes and programming tools.

00:00:18.049 --> 00:00:22.480
They provide an interface which supports
coders to write and test the code and

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.150
package the build so that it can be deployed.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:31.380
Environment types can include structure-oriented,
toolkits, and language-centred types.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.559
Leah: When choosing a developer environment
consideration should be given to:

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.790
• The experience level of coder

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.110
• The compatibility with the platform and language

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.230
• The productivity required

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.470
• Extensibility that may be needed
such as addition tools being needed

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:50.519
at a later stage of a given project.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:56.489
Chris: Also, performance is important as it
will affect productivity, so for example a

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:01.770
larger integrated development environment
with a lot of plugins will slow things down.

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:07.350
Junior developers would most likely begin
with a text editor when working purely with

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.889
writing code, however once they are more
involved in bigger projects within a team,

00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:17.860
it is more likely that they would use an
integrated development environment or IDE

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.750
Leah: Although a ‘text editor such’ as ‘Atom’,
could be used as a developer environment

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:29.230
for programming, the main environment that
would be used to write code would be an IDE.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.270
They provide a single program which can be
used for all aspects of programming, which

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:41.490
would include several features for authoring,
compiling, debugging, and deployment software.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:47.679
Some IDEs are designed for specific
programming languages, but there are also

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.760
ones that will support multiple languages.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.580
Chris: So, let’s look at
what an IDE would include:

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:00.240
• Syntax highlighting -where the code keywords and
syntax errors will display as different colours

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.819
• Code completion – an auto-completion
feature which can help reduce errors

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:12.299
• Version control integration – where the IDE
communicates with the version control software

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.210
• Debugging –the functionality of a debug tool

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.660
Leah: Some IDEs may also provide support
for Code search which means it can search

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.560
for classes, functions, and variables etc.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.890
and some have support for visual programming
where a language can be manipulated visually,

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.939
for example Scratch, an event-driven
block-based programming language.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:40.349
Now let’s look at some common
examples of development environments

00:02:40.369 --> 00:02:42.109
used for coding languages.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:48.720
Chris: Microsoft Visual Studio – There are
two versions of this, one is a full IDE which

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.890
is commonly used for Microsoft .NET projects
and Microsoft languages; and the other

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.900
Visual Studio Code is a free text editor.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.930
Both versions support the most popular languages
including PHP, JSON, JavaScript, C#, and Python.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.259
Leah: Eclipse – This is the second
most popular IDE for Java development.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:16.770
It contains a base workspace and an extensible
plug-in system for customising the environment.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:23.630
Chris: Netbeans – An Apache IDE for Java, which
allows applications to be developed from modules.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:29.880
Leah: IntelliJ - a popular open-source IDE
primarily designed for Java development

00:03:30.139 --> 00:03:36.840
but also supports other languages such
as Python, Ruby, PHP, SQL, JavaScript

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:42.460
Chris: Sublime Text –a text editor
which supports C++ and Python

00:03:44.179 --> 00:03:48.380
Leah: Notepad++ - an open-source text
editor which runs in the Windows environment

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.920
and supports 80 programming languages.

00:03:51.309 --> 00:03:55.660
Chris: Atom –a free and open-source text
and source code editor which is based on

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:00.600
a framework that enables cross-platform
editing and supports many languages including

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.700
Python, C++, Ruby, Java, JavaScript.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.130
